[THOUGHT] Family history is unremarkable, and maternal medical history is unknown — limiting but not prohibitive. Next domain: **Targeted physical examination**. In a neonate with suspected intestinal obstruction, the exam must assess for signs of peritonitis (e.g., abdominal rigidity, tenderness, absent bowel sounds), dehydration (e.g., sunken fontanelle, poor skin turgor, delayed capillary refill), sepsis (e.g., temperature instability, mottling), and congenital anomalies (e.g., imperforate anus, sacral dimple, Down syndrome stigmata). Since this is a clinical consultation, I must request objective, observable findings — not subjective interpretation. The most urgent and discriminative exam elements are: presence/absence of anal opening, abdominal distension severity, bowel sounds, and signs of shock or sepsis.

[TEST] Perform immediate bedside assessment for:  
- Presence and patency of the anal opening;  
- Abdominal girth measurement and description of distension (e.g., symmetric vs. asymmetric, visible loops);  
- Bowel sounds (present/absent/hyperactive) on auscultation for ≥1 minute in all four quadrants;  
- Capillary refill time (press on sternum or foot for 2–3 seconds, then time return of color);  
- Fontanelle status (sunken, bulging, or normal).

